      <property key="dct:title" value="Quality Control Authority Business-actor"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA team"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="1. Overseeing the implementation of tax regulations: A Quality Control Authority ensures that all tax laws and regulations are being properly implemented. They monitor the processes and systems used to collect and manage taxes, ensuring they are efficient, effective, and in compliance with all relevant laws.&#xA;&#xA;2. Auditing and inspection: The authority conducts regular audits and inspections of businesses and individuals to ensure they are correctly reporting their income and paying the appropriate amount of tax. They also inspect the work of tax officials to ensure they are performing their duties correctly.&#xA;&#xA;3. Handling complaints and disputes: If a taxpayer has a complaint or dispute about their tax assessment or the way they have been treated by tax officials, the Quality Control Authority investigates and resolves the issue. They ensure that all taxpayers are treated fairly and that their rights are protected.&#xA;&#xA;4. Training and development: The authority is responsible for the training and development of tax officials. They ensure that all officials are up-to-date with the latest tax laws and regulations, and that they have the skills and knowledge to perform their duties effectively.&#xA;&#xA;5. Policy development: The Quality Control Authority plays a key role in the development of tax policy. They analyze the effectiveness of current policies and propose changes or improvements based on their findings. They also provide expert advice to government officials on tax matters.&#xA;&#xA;6. Ensuring transparency and accountability: The authority ensures that the tax system is transparent and accountable. They publish regular reports on their activities and the performance of the tax system, and they are accountable to the public and to government officials for their actions."/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="The Quality Control Authority is a critical business role responsible for ensuring that all processes, systems, and outputs meet the established standards of quality, efficiency, and compliance. This role is particularly crucial in the context of tax regulations, where adherence to legal and procedural guidelines is paramount.&#xA;&#xA;The Quality Control Authority's primary responsibility is to develop, implement, and manage the quality control systems. These systems are designed to ensure that all tax-related processes, from the collection and processing of tax data to the calculation and payment of taxes, are carried out accurately, efficiently, and in full compliance with all relevant regulations.&#xA;&#xA;The Quality Control Authority is also responsible for conducting regular audits and inspections of the tax processes. This involves reviewing the procedures and systems in place, checking the accuracy of tax calculations, and verifying that all tax payments and filings are made on time and in the correct manner. If any issues or discrepancies are identified, the Quality Control Authority is responsible for taking corrective action to resolve them.&#xA;&#xA;In addition, the Quality Control Authority plays a key role in risk management. This involves identifying potential risks and vulnerabilities in the tax processes, and implementing measures to mitigate these risks. This could include strengthening security measures, improving data accuracy and integrity, or enhancing procedural compliance.&#xA;&#xA;Furthermore, the Quality Control Authority is responsible for training and educating staff on quality control procedures and standards. This includes providing guidance on how to comply with tax regulations, how to use the tax systems and tools effectively, and how to identify and resolve quality issues.&#xA;&#xA;Finally, the Quality Control Authority is responsible for reporting on the performance of the quality control systems. This involves tracking key performance indicators, analyzing trends and patterns, and providing regular reports to senior management. These reports provide valuable insights into the effectiveness of the quality control systems, and help to inform decision-making and strategic planning.&#xA;&#xA;In the ArchiMate business role context, the Quality Control Authority is a role that interacts with other roles, processes, and systems within the organization. This role is linked to various business processes, such as tax calculation and payment, and interacts with other roles, such as tax officers and accountants, to ensure that all tax-related activities are carried out in accordance with the established quality standards."/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="The Quality Control Authority role is responsible for ensuring that all tax-related processes, procedures, and systems comply with established European tax regulations and standards. This role involves monitoring and evaluating the efficiency and effectiveness of tax operations, identifying areas of non-compliance or potential improvement, and implementing corrective measures or enhancements as necessary. The Quality Control Authority also plays a crucial role in mitigating tax-related risks, ensuring data accuracy, and promoting a culture of continuous improvement and regulatory compliance within the organization."/>
