    <element xsi:type="archimate:BusinessActor" name="Statistical authorities" id="id-fb42726e55614c788225d98fa952b650">
      <documentation>Definition: The Statistical Authority's role is to collect, process, and disseminate statistical data in accordance with the European Taxes Regulation. They are responsible for ensuring the accuracy, reliability, and timeliness of the data, as well as its compliance with the standards and guidelines set by the regulation. As an ArchiMate business role, the Statistical Authority also oversees the coordination and integration of statistical activities, the development of statistical methodologies, and the provision of advice and guidance on statistical matters. They play a crucial role in supporting decision-making, policy formulation, and monitoring and evaluation processes related to taxation.

Source: EIRA team

Additional information: The Statistical Authority's role is to collect, process, analyze, and disseminate statistical data that is relevant and necessary for various sectors, including the taxation sector. This role is crucial in ensuring that all statistical information is accurate, reliable, and timely, which is essential for decision-making processes, policy formulation, and strategic planning.

In the context of taxation, the Statistical Authority is responsible for gathering and analyzing data related to taxes. This includes data on tax revenues, tax rates, tax structures, and the impact of taxes on different sectors of the economy. The authority is also responsible for monitoring and evaluating tax policies and their effects on economic growth, income distribution, and other key economic indicators.

The Statistical Authority also plays a key role in ensuring compliance with tax regulations. This involves collecting and analyzing data on tax compliance and evasion, and providing this information to relevant bodies for enforcement and policy-making purposes.

Furthermore, the Statistical Authority is responsible for maintaining and updating statistical databases and systems, ensuring their security and confidentiality, and providing access to statistical data for users in a user-friendly and accessible format. This includes providing technical support and guidance to users, and conducting training and capacity-building activities to enhance the use and understanding of statistical data.

As an Archimate business role, the Statistical Authority is also responsible for managing and coordinating the activities of various entities involved in the collection, processing, analysis, and dissemination of statistical data. This includes coordinating with other statistical agencies, government departments, research institutions, and other stakeholders to ensure the consistency, comparability, and quality of statistical data.

The Statistical Authority is also expected to adhere to international standards and best practices in statistical production and dissemination, and to continuously improve its methodologies and processes to enhance the quality and relevance of statistical data. This includes conducting research and development activities, and participating in international statistical forums and networks.

Example: 1. Data Collection: Statistical authorities are responsible for collecting data on various aspects of the economy, including income, expenditure, production, employment, and prices. This data is essential for calculating tax bases and determining tax rates.

2. Data Analysis: After collecting data, statistical authorities analyze it to identify trends, patterns, and anomalies. This analysis can inform tax policy decisions, such as whether to raise or lower tax rates, or to introduce new taxes.

3. Economic Forecasting: Statistical authorities use the data they collect and analyze to make economic forecasts. These forecasts can help policymakers anticipate future tax revenues and plan accordingly.

4. Compliance Monitoring: Statistical authorities monitor compliance with tax laws. They use statistical methods to identify potential cases of tax evasion or fraud, and to assess the effectiveness of enforcement efforts.

5. Policy Evaluation: Statistical authorities evaluate the impact of tax policies. They use statistical methods to measure the effects of tax changes on the economy, and to assess whether the policies are achieving their intended goals.

6. Research and Development: Statistical authorities conduct research to improve their methods of data collection, analysis, and forecasting. They also develop new statistical tools and techniques to better understand the economy and the tax system.

7. Education and Communication: Statistical authorities educate the public and policymakers about the economy and the tax system. They communicate their findings in a clear and understandable way, and they provide information that can help people make informed decisions about tax matters. 

8. Collaboration: Statistical authorities collaborate with other government agencies, international organizations, and academic institutions. They share data, exchange ideas, and work together on joint projects. This collaboration can enhance the quality and usefulness of the statistical information that they produce.

LOST view: OV-Digital Public Service Consumers

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/StatisticalAuthoritiesBusinessActor

EIRA traceability: eira:DigitalPublicServiceDeliveryConsumerBusinessRole

ABB name: egovera:StatisticalAuthoritiesBusinessActor

EIRA concept: eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2023-07-27

dct:identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/StatisticalAuthoritiesBusinessActor

dct:title: Statistical Authorities Business-actor


Definition: The Statistical Authority's role is to collect, process, and disseminate statistical data in accordance with the European Taxes Regulation. They are responsible for ensuring the accuracy, reliability, and timeliness of the data, as well as its compliance with the standards and guidelines set by the regulation. As an ArchiMate business role, the Statistical Authority also oversees the coordination and integration of statistical activities, the development of statistical methodologies, and the provision of advice and guidance on statistical matters. They play a crucial role in supporting decision-making, policy formulation, and monitoring and evaluation processes related to taxation.

Source: EIRA team

Additional information: The Statistical Authority's role is to collect, process, analyze, and disseminate statistical data that is relevant and necessary for various sectors, including the taxation sector. This role is crucial in ensuring that all statistical information is accurate, reliable, and timely, which is essential for decision-making processes, policy formulation, and strategic planning.

In the context of taxation, the Statistical Authority is responsible for gathering and analyzing data related to taxes. This includes data on tax revenues, tax rates, tax structures, and the impact of taxes on different sectors of the economy. The authority is also responsible for monitoring and evaluating tax policies and their effects on economic growth, income distribution, and other key economic indicators.

The Statistical Authority also plays a key role in ensuring compliance with tax regulations. This involves collecting and analyzing data on tax compliance and evasion, and providing this information to relevant bodies for enforcement and policy-making purposes.

Furthermore, the Statistical Authority is responsible for maintaining and updating statistical databases and systems, ensuring their security and confidentiality, and providing access to statistical data for users in a user-friendly and accessible format. This includes providing technical support and guidance to users, and conducting training and capacity-building activities to enhance the use and understanding of statistical data.

As an Archimate business role, the Statistical Authority is also responsible for managing and coordinating the activities of various entities involved in the collection, processing, analysis, and dissemination of statistical data. This includes coordinating with other statistical agencies, government departments, research institutions, and other stakeholders to ensure the consistency, comparability, and quality of statistical data.

The Statistical Authority is also expected to adhere to international standards and best practices in statistical production and dissemination, and to continuously improve its methodologies and processes to enhance the quality and relevance of statistical data. This includes conducting research and development activities, and participating in international statistical forums and networks.

Example: 1. Data Collection: Statistical authorities are responsible for collecting data on various aspects of the economy, including income, expenditure, production, employment, and prices. This data is essential for calculating tax bases and determining tax rates.

2. Data Analysis: After collecting data, statistical authorities analyze it to identify trends, patterns, and anomalies. This analysis can inform tax policy decisions, such as whether to raise or lower tax rates, or to introduce new taxes.

3. Economic Forecasting: Statistical authorities use the data they collect and analyze to make economic forecasts. These forecasts can help policymakers anticipate future tax revenues and plan accordingly.

4. Compliance Monitoring: Statistical authorities monitor compliance with tax laws. They use statistical methods to identify potential cases of tax evasion or fraud, and to assess the effectiveness of enforcement efforts.

5. Policy Evaluation: Statistical authorities evaluate the impact of tax policies. They use statistical methods to measure the effects of tax changes on the economy, and to assess whether the policies are achieving their intended goals.

6. Research and Development: Statistical authorities conduct research to improve their methods of data collection, analysis, and forecasting. They also develop new statistical tools and techniques to better understand the economy and the tax system.

7. Education and Communication: Statistical authorities educate the public and policymakers about the economy and the tax system. They communicate their findings in a clear and understandable way, and they provide information that can help people make informed decisions about tax matters. 

8. Collaboration: Statistical authorities collaborate with other government agencies, international organizations, and academic institutions. They share data, exchange ideas, and work together on joint projects. This collaboration can enhance the quality and usefulness of the statistical information that they produce.

LOST view: OV-Digital Public Service Consumers

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/StatisticalAuthoritiesBusinessActor

EIRA traceability: eira:DigitalPublicServiceDeliveryConsumerBusinessRole

