DPR) in Europe.&#xA;&#xA;6. Privacy by Design: Access rights are an essential component of the privacy by design principle, which is a fundamental aspect of European data protection laws. By implementing access rights from the early stages of system design, organizations can ensure that privacy and data protection measures are integrated into their IT systems, minimizing the risk of unauthorized access and data breaches.&#xA;&#xA;Overall, access rights are a critical element in the European IT context, enabling organizations to protect sensitive information, comply with data protection regulations, and safeguard the privacy of individuals."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="Access rights, in the context of information, refer to the permissions and privileges granted to individuals or entities to access and manipulate specific data or resources within an information system. It is a crucial component of information management and security, ensuring that only authorized users can access, modify, or delete information.&#xA;&#xA;Access rights are designed to protect sensitive and confidential data, prevent unauthorized access, and maintain the integrity and availability of information. These rights are typically implemented through access control mechanisms, such as user authentication, authorization, and audit trails, which are enforced by the underlying IT infrastructure.&#xA;&#xA;In the European IT context, access rights are governed by various regulations and frameworks, such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and the Network and Information Security (NIS) Directive. These regulations aim to protect the privacy and security of personal data and ensure that organizations handle information in a responsible and compliant manner.&#xA;&#xA;Access rights can be categorized into different levels or types, depending on the level of access granted to users. These may include:&#xA;&#xA;1. Read Access: This level allows users to view or read information but does not permit them to make any changes or modifications. It is typically granted to individuals who require access to information for reference or informational purposes.&#xA;&#xA;2. Write Access: Users with write access have the ability to create, modify, or delete information within the system. This level of access is usually granted to authorized personnel responsible for updating or maintaining data.&#xA;&#xA;3. Execute Access: Execute access allows users to run or execute specific programs, scripts, or commands within the system. It is commonly granted to individuals who need to perform specific tasks or operations within the IT environment.&#xA;&#xA;4. Delete Access: This level of access permits users to remove or delete information from the system. It is typically restricted to authorized personnel who are responsible for data management and retention.&#xA;&#xA;5. Administrative Access: Administrative access provides users with elevated privileges, allowing them to manage and configure the system, grant or revoke access rights, and perform other administrative tasks. This level of access is usually limited to IT administrators or system administrators.&#xA;&#xA;Access rights are typically assigned based on the principle of least privilege, which means that users are granted the minimum level of access necessary to perform their job responsibilities. This principle helps minimize the risk of unauthorized access, data breaches, and misuse of information.&#xA;&#xA;To ensure effective access rights management, organizations often implement access control mechanisms, such as role-based access control (RBAC) or attribute-based access control (ABAC). These mechanisms enable organizations to define access policies, roles, and permissions, ensuring that users only have access to the information they need to perform their tasks.&#xA;&#xA;Regular monitoring, auditing, and review of access rights are essential to maintain the security and integrity of information. Organizations must regularly assess and update access rights to reflect changes in job roles, responsibilities, and organizational structure, as well as comply with legal and regulatory requirements."/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="Access rights are data that determine which users can view the object and what those users can do."/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="OV-Functional Architecture Principles"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="Organisational view [Motivation]"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="OV-Data Spaces [Motivation]"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="OV-Data Spaces"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="SV-Agnostic Data Policies and Contracts- Data Space"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="Semantic view - Motivation"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="Organisational view"/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="Organisational"/>
      <property key="skos:broader" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/InformationBusinessObject"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:BusinessObject" name="Audit information" id="id-6d4d1da240b54eb99b6f755b455fdce3">
      <documentation>Definition: Audit information, as a component of information, refers to the data collected and analyzed during an audit process. This information includes detailed records, transaction histories, procedures, and controls within an IT system. It is used to assess the system's effectiveness, efficiency, and compliance with relevant regulations and standards. The audit information helps in identifying any discrepancies, weaknesses, or potential risks in the system, thereby enabling the implementation of corrective measures to improve system security, integrity, and performance.

Source: EIRA team

Additional information: Audit information, as an element or component of information, refers to the data collected and analyzed during an audit process. This information is crucial in assessing the efficiency, effectiveness, and compliance of an organization's processes, systems, and operations. It includes details about the organization's operations, financial transactions, business processes, and internal controls.

In the context of Information Technology (IT), audit information involves the examination and evaluation of an organization's IT infrastructure, operations, and policies. It includes data related to the organization's IT systems, applications, and networks. This information is used to determine whether the IT systems are safeguarding the organization's assets, maintaining data integrity, and operating effectively to achieve the organization's goals or objectives.

Audit information can be both qualitative and quantitative. Qualitative audit information may include descriptions of processes, systems, controls, and risk management practices. Quantitative audit information, on the other hand, may include numerical data such as financial figures, performance metrics, and statistical analyses.

The collection and analysis of audit information involve various methods such as interviews, observations, document reviews, and testing. The results of these methods are then documented and reported to provide an objective and independent assessment of the organization's operations and controls.

In the IT context, audit information may also include data related to cybersecurity, data privacy, and data protection. This information is crucial in assessing the organization's compliance with relevant laws, regulations, and standards.

Overall, audit information is a critical component of the audit process. It provides the basis for the auditor's findings, conclusions, and recommendations, and it helps stakeholders make informed decisions about the organization's operations and controls.

Example: 1. Financial Sector: In the financial sector, audit information is used to ensure that all financial transactions and processes are carried out correctly and in accordance with the law. For example, a bank may use audit information to verify that all loans have been properly approved and that all payments have been correctly recorded. This helps to prevent fraud and ensure the accuracy of financial records.

2. Healthcare Sector: In the healthcare sector, audit information can be used to ensure that all patient data is handled correctly and that all medical procedures are carried out in accordance with the relevant guidelines. For example, a hospital may use audit information to verify that all patient records are kept confidential and that all surgeries are performed by qualified professionals. This helps to ensure the quality of healthcare services and protect patient rights.

3. Retail Sector: In the retail sector, audit information can be used to track inventory and ensure that all sales transactions are correctly recorded. For example, a supermarket may use audit information to verify that all products are properly stocked and that all sales are accurately recorded. This helps to prevent theft and ensure the accuracy of sales records.

4. Manufacturing Sector: In the manufacturing sector, audit information can be used to ensure that all production processes are carried out correctly and that all products meet the required quality standards. For example, a car manufacturer may use audit information to verify that all cars are assembled correctly and that all parts are of the required quality. This helps to ensure the quality of products and prevent defects.

5. Public Sector: In the public sector, audit information can be used to ensure that all public funds are used correctly and that all public services are delivered efficiently. For example, a local government may use audit information to verify that all public funds are spent on the intended purposes and that all public services are delivered to the required standards. This helps to ensure the efficient use of public funds and the quality of public services.

LOST view: OV-Information Base

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/AuditInformationBusinessObject

EIRA traceability: eira:InformationBusinessObject

ABB name: egovera:AuditInformationBusinessObject

EIRA concept: eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2023-07-27

