</documentation>
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/BankingDataBusinessObject"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/BankingDataBusinessObject"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:BankingDataBusinessObject"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="Banking data Information"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA team"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="1. Fraud Detection: Banking data is extensively used in fraud detection systems. By analyzing patterns and anomalies in transaction data, banks can identify suspicious activities and take necessary actions to prevent fraudulent transactions. For example, if a customer who usually uses their card in Germany suddenly makes a purchase in Spain, the system can flag this as potential fraud.&#xA;&#xA;2. Credit Scoring: Banks use customer data to assess their creditworthiness. This includes data on income, employment, existing debts, and repayment history. Based on this information, banks can calculate a credit score which helps them decide whether to approve or reject a loan application.&#xA;&#xA;3. Personalized Marketing: Banks use customer data to understand their preferences and behavior. This information can be used to tailor marketing campaigns and offer personalized products and services. For example, if a bank knows that a customer frequently travels abroad, they might offer them a credit card with no foreign transaction fees.&#xA;&#xA;4. Risk Management: Banks use data to assess and manage various types of risks, including credit risk, market risk, and operational risk. For example, by analyzing historical data, banks can predict the likelihood of a customer defaulting on their loan. This helps them make informed decisions and mitigate potential losses.&#xA;&#xA;5. Regulatory Compliance: Banks are required to comply with various regulations to ensure financial stability and protect consumers. This includes reporting certain types of data to regulatory authorities. For example, banks need to report data on their capital adequacy, which is a measure of their ability to absorb losses.&#xA;&#xA;6. Customer Service: Banks use customer data to improve their service. By understanding a customer's needs and preferences, banks can provide a more personalized and efficient service. For example, if a customer frequently makes international transfers, the bank might suggest them to use their specialized international transfer service.&#xA;&#xA;7. Business Intelligence: Banks use data to gain insights into their operations and make strategic decisions. This includes data on their performance, market trends, and customer behavior. For example, by analyzing transaction data, a bank might identify that a particular branch is underperforming and take necessary actions to improve its performance."/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="Banking data refers to the vast and diverse array of information generated, collected, processed, and stored by banking institutions in the course of their operations. This data encompasses a wide range of elements, including but not limited to, personal customer information, transaction details, account balances, credit history, risk profiles, and investment data.&#xA;&#xA;Personal customer information includes data such as names, addresses, contact details, and identification numbers. This data is crucial for customer identification, communication, and compliance with legal requirements.&#xA;&#xA;Transaction details refer to the data related to all the financial transactions carried out by the customers. This includes the date, time, amount, and nature of the transaction, the parties involved, and the channels used. This data is essential for tracking and monitoring purposes, fraud detection, and financial reporting.&#xA;&#xA;Account balances provide information about the amount of money held by customers in their various accounts at any given point in time. This data is vital for managing customer relationships, assessing creditworthiness, and ensuring regulatory compliance.&#xA;&#xA;Credit history data provides information about the borrowing and repayment behavior of customers. This includes data on loans taken, repayment schedules, defaults, and credit scores. This data is critical for assessing credit risk and making lending decisions.&#xA;&#xA;Risk profiles include data on the various risks associated with each customer or transaction. This includes credit risk, market risk, operational risk, and liquidity risk. This data is crucial for risk management and regulatory compliance.&#xA;&#xA;Investment data includes information about the investments made by the bank or its customers. This includes data on the type of investment, the amount invested, the return on investment, and the risk associated with the investment. This data is essential for investment management and financial planning.&#xA;&#xA;In the European context, banking data is subject to stringent regulations to ensure data privacy, data security, and data governance. Banks are required to comply with regulations such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), which mandates strict controls over the collection, processing, and storage of personal data. They are also required to implement robust data security measures to protect against data breaches and cyber threats. Furthermore, banks are required to have robust data governance frameworks in place to ensure the accuracy, completeness, and integrity of their data."/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="Banking data refers to the comprehensive information related to a bank's operations, including but not limited to, customer details, transaction history, account information, loan details, and investment records. It is a crucial component of a bank's information system, enabling them to track, analyze, and manage their operations effectively. This data is subject to stringent data protection regulations to ensure the privacy and security of customer information. In the digital age, banking data also plays a pivotal role in developing personalized services, enhancing customer experience, and implementing AI-driven solutions for risk assessment and fraud detection."/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="OV-Information Base"/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
