      <property key="skos:example" value="Payment is a crucial element or component of information systems in various applications within the European IT context. Here are a few examples:&#xA;&#xA;1. E-commerce platforms: In online shopping websites or mobile applications, payment systems are integrated to facilitate secure and convenient transactions. These systems enable customers to make payments using various methods such as credit/debit cards, digital wallets, or bank transfers. They ensure the confidentiality and integrity of payment information, protect against fraud, and provide real-time transaction processing.&#xA;&#xA;2. Mobile banking applications: With the rise of smartphones, mobile banking applications have become popular in Europe. These apps allow users to perform various financial activities, including making payments. Users can transfer funds to other individuals or businesses, pay bills, or make purchases using their mobile devices. Payment information is securely stored and transmitted, adhering to European data protection regulations.&#xA;&#xA;3. Peer-to-peer payment platforms: Peer-to-peer (P2P) payment platforms enable individuals to send and receive money directly from their bank accounts or digital wallets. These platforms are commonly used for splitting bills, repaying debts, or sending money to friends and family. Payment information is securely processed, and users can often add additional security measures such as two-factor authentication.&#xA;&#xA;4. Subscription-based services: Many European IT companies offer subscription-based services, such as streaming platforms, software-as-a-service (SaaS) applications, or online publications. Payment systems are integrated into these services to handle recurring payments from subscribers. Payment information is securely stored and processed, ensuring seamless and uninterrupted access to the subscribed services.&#xA;&#xA;5. Contactless payments: In Europe, contactless payment methods, such as N