    <element xsi:type="archimate:BusinessService" name="Revenue tracking and analysis" id="id-157e1909f0f34d01899b7e11441bae20">
      <documentation>Definition: Revenue tracking and analysis is a crucial IT concept that involves the use of technology to monitor, analyze, and report on an organization's income. It provides insights into the financial health of a business, helping to identify trends, forecast future revenues, and make informed decisions.

Source: Investopedia

Source reference: https://www.investopedia.com/terms/r/revenue.asp

Additional information: Revenue tracking and analysis is a comprehensive process that involves tracking every single transaction that brings in revenue to the company. This includes sales, returns, discounts, and any other sources of income. The data collected is then analyzed to identify patterns and trends, which can be used to make strategic business decisions. This process is often automated using various IT tools and software, which can collect and analyze large amounts of data quickly and accurately. It's an essential part of financial management, budgeting, and strategic planning in any organization.

Example: Examples of revenue tracking and analysis include a retail store using a point-of-sale system to track daily sales and identify the most popular products, or a SaaS company using analytics software to monitor subscription renewals and cancellations. Another example is an e-commerce website using tracking tools to monitor the effectiveness of different marketing campaigns in driving sales.

LOST view: OV-Digital Public Services Catalogue

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/RevenueTrackingAndAnalysisBusinessService

EIRA traceability: eira:DigitalPublicBusinessService

ABB name: egovera:RevenueTrackingAndAnalysisBusinessService

EIRA concept: eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2023-07-27

dct:identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/RevenueTrackingAndAnalysisBusinessService

dct:title: Revenue tracking and analysis Digital Public Service

</documentation>
