</documentation>
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/InterestsAndPenaltiesBusinessService"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/InterestsAndPenaltiesBusinessService"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="Interests and penalties Digital Public Service"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:InterestsAndPenaltiesBusinessService"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="Interests and penalties in IT refer to the additional charges or deductions applied to a user or a system for non-compliance with the terms and conditions or for late payments. These are often used in software licensing, cloud services, and online subscriptions."/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="Techopedia"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference" value="https://www.techopedia.com/definition/2383/software-license"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="1. A company using a SaaS product fails to pay the subscription fee by the due date. The service provider charges a late fee as a penalty. 2. A user exceeds their allocated storage limit on a cloud service. The service provider charges an additional fee (interest) for the extra storage used."/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="In the context of IT, interests and penalties are often financial in nature and are designed to enforce compliance with the terms and conditions of a service. For instance, a software license may stipulate that late payment of the license fee will result in a penalty. Similarly, a cloud service provider may charge interest on late payments. These penalties and interests are often calculated as a percentage of the amount due and are added to the total amount payable. They serve as a deterrent to late payment and non-compliance, and as a means for the service provider to recoup some of the costs associated with managing and chasing late payments."/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="OV-Digital Public Services Catalogue"/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="Organisational"/>
      <property key="eira:businessDomain" value="taxes"/>
      <property key="skos:broader" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/DigitalPublicBusinessService"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:BusinessService" name="Intangible assets registry management" id="id-f68fcf52766b4e78a474379b06d32ffc">
      <documentation>Definition: Intangible Assets Registry Management is an IT concept that involves the use of software or systems to track, manage, and optimize the use of an organization's intangible assets. These assets may include intellectual property, brand equity, customer relationships, and proprietary technology.

Source: Investopedia

Source reference: https://www.investopedia.com/terms/i/intangibleasset.asp

Additional information: Intangible Assets Registry Management is crucial for businesses as it helps in the identification, valuation, and reporting of intangible assets which are not physically present but contribute significantly to a company's value. These assets can be anything from patents, copyrights, trademarks, brand recognition, customer lists, proprietary technology, etc. The management of these assets involves keeping a record of their existence, their ownership details, their value, and their lifespan. This information is vital for strategic planning, financial reporting, and decision-making processes within an organization. The use of IT in managing these assets makes the process more efficient, accurate, and reliable.

Example: Examples of application include a software company using an intangible assets registry management system to keep track of its software patents, their value, and their expiration dates. Another example could be a marketing firm using the system to manage its client relationships and brand equity. In the financial sector, banks could use it to manage their customer data and proprietary algorithms.

LOST view: OV-Digital Public Services Catalogue

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/IntangibleAssetsRegistryManagementBusinessService

