    <element xsi:type="archimate:Grouping" name="Observability and Monitoring Enablers" id="id-147d4bd4fcf8466f876626c8d55ada1c">
      <documentation>Definition: Observability and Monitoring Enablers ABB is a Grouping that refers to components and services that facilitate the collection, analysis, and interpretation of data to gain insights into the performance, health, and potential issues of various systems, networks, applications, and services. It includes the concepts of Observability and Monitoring, which involve tracking and understanding the internal state and behavior of a system through its external outputs, and observing and tracking the performance and health of IT infrastructure, respectively.

Source: EIRA Team Research

Source reference: N/A

Additional information: Observability and Monitoring Enablers ABB is a crucial aspect of IT operations, as it allows for the proactive identification and resolution of issues, ensuring optimal performance and reliability of IT infrastructure. Observability involves the collection and analysis of various metrics, logs, traces, and other data points, providing insights into the system's performance, health, and potential issues. On the other hand, Monitoring involves observing and tracking the performance, availability, and health of systems, networks, applications, and services, generating alerts or reports to facilitate informed decision-making and troubleshooting.

Example: Examples of Observability and Monitoring Enablers ABB in action include the use of application performance monitoring (APM) tools to track and analyze the performance of software applications, network monitoring tools to observe and manage network performance and reliability, and log management solutions to collect, analyze, and visualize log data for insights into system behavior and potential issues.

LOST view: TVA-Observability and Monitoring Enablers [Motivation]

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/ObservabilityAndMonitoringEnablersGrouping

ABB name: egovera:ObservabilityAndMonitoringEnablersGrouping

EIRA concept: eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2023-08-17

dct:identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/ObservabilityAndMonitoringEnablersGrouping

dct:title: Observability And Monitoring EnablersGrouping

</documentation>
      <property key="eira:PURI" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/ObservabilityAndMonitoringEnablersGrouping"/>
      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/ObservabilityAndMonitoringEnablersGrouping"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="Observability And Monitoring EnablersGrouping"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:ObservabilityAndMonitoringEnablersGrouping"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="Observability and Monitoring Enablers ABB is a Grouping that refers to components and services that facilitate the collection, analysis, and interpretation of data to gain insights into the performance, health, and potential issues of various systems, networks, applications, and services. It includes the concepts of Observability and Monitoring, which involve tracking and understanding the internal state and behavior of a system through its external outputs, and observing and tracking the performance and health of IT infrastructure, respectively."/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA Team Research"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference" value="N/A"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="Examples of Observability and Monitoring Enablers ABB in action include the use of application performance monitoring (APM) tools to track and analyze the performance of software applications, network monitoring tools to observe and manage network performance and reliability, and log management solutions to collect, analyze, and visualize log data for insights into system behavior and potential issues."/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="Observability and Monitoring Enablers ABB is a crucial aspect of IT operations, as it allows for the proactive identification and resolution of issues, ensuring optimal performance and reliability of IT infrastructure. Observability involves the collection and analysis of various metrics, logs, traces, and other data points, providing insights into the system's performance, health, and potential issues. On the other hand, Monitoring involves observing and tracking the performance, availability, and health of systems, networks, applications, and services, generating alerts or reports to facilitate informed decision-making and troubleshooting."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="TVA-Observability and Monitoring Enablers [Motivation]"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="TVI-Networking Infrastructure Enablers [Motivation]"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="Technical view - application"/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="TechnicalApplication"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Grouping" name="Business Agnostic Organisational Agreement Catalogue" id="id-15cd0c5ce9e94aee96cece65e2b987de">
      <documentation>Definition: Business Agnostic Organisational Agreement Catalogue ABB is a grouping that refers to the actual catalogue of specifications, agreements and/or contracts that facilitates the establishment and implementation of national governance models. It serves as a repository of the necessary documents that guide the coordination and collaboration among various government agencies, departments, and stakeholders in developing and implementing common standards, policies, and guidelines for IT interoperability within the European Union.

Source: EIRA Team Research

Additional information: The Business Agnostic Organisational Agreement Catalogue ABB is a critical tool in promoting seamless interoperability, data security, and efficient digital services within a country. It contains the necessary specifications, agreements, and contracts that define the framework and processes for IT interoperability. This includes the roles and responsibilities of different stakeholders, the standards and policies to be followed, and the guidelines for data exchange and sharing. The catalogue is 'business agnostic', meaning it is applicable regardless of the specific business or industry involved. It aligns with the broader European IT interoperability framework, facilitating cross-border data exchange and cooperation.

Example: An example of the application of the Business Agnostic Organisational Agreement Catalogue ABB could be a country developing its National Governance Model for IT interoperability. The country would refer to the catalogue to understand the necessary specifications, agreements, and contracts to be established. This could include agreements on data sharing between government departments, contracts with IT service providers to ensure they adhere to the required standards, and specifications for the IT systems to be used. The catalogue would guide the country in ensuring its National Governance Model aligns with the broader European IT interoperability framework.

LOST view: OV-Governance Architecture Principles

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/BusinessAgnosticOrganisationalAgreementCatalogueGrouping

EIRA traceability: eira:TechnicalAgreementContract

