      <property key="dct:title" value="Legislation On Behavioural Interoperability (NNBI) Catalogue"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:LegislationOnBehaviouralInteroperabilityGrouping"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NNBI) is a grouping that covers a range of legal and regulatory frameworks, policies, and guidelines that aim to facilitate and govern the interaction and exchange of information between different systems, applications, and components in a way that they can operate together effectively and efficiently. This catalogue does not contain any specific elements as indicated by the provided JSON."/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA Team Research"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="An example of a legislation that could be included in the Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NNBI) is a law that sets standards for data exchange between different healthcare systems, ensuring that patient information can be shared seamlessly and securely between different healthcare providers. Another example could be a regulation that defines the protocols for communication between different components of a smart city, ensuring that they can work together to optimize city operations and services."/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="The Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NNBI) is a theoretical catalogue that would encompass various laws, regulations, and guidelines that are designed to ensure the smooth interaction and exchange of information between different systems, applications, and components. These laws and regulations would aim to ensure that these different entities can work together in a seamless and efficient manner, without any barriers or obstacles. This would involve setting standards for data exchange, defining protocols for communication, and establishing guidelines for system design and operation. However, the provided JSON indicates that this catalogue does not currently contain any specific elements."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="LV-Architecture Principles"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="LV-Binding Power and Jurisdiction"/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="Legal"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Grouping" name="Tax Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NBI)" id="id-2902cce806944bc2aeb9ed22df1a79ae">
      <documentation>Definition: Tax Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NBI) ABB is a grouping that covers the legal frameworks, rules, and regulations that govern the sharing and use of open data in the context of tax legislation. This includes the principles, policies, and standards that ensure the effective exchange and integration of data between different systems, applications, and entities within the tax legislative environment.

Source: EIRA Team Research

Additional information: The Tax Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NBI) ABB primarily focuses on open data legislation, which refers to the laws and regulations that promote transparency, accountability, and value creation by making tax-related data freely available and usable. This involves the provision of data in a manner that is machine-readable, freely shared, and with minimal restrictions on use, reuse, and redistribution. The aim is to facilitate interoperability, which is the ability of different information systems, devices, or applications to access, exchange, integrate, and cooperatively use data in a coordinated manner, within and across organizational, regional, and national boundaries, to provide timely and seamless portability of information and optimize the health and efficiency of the overall system.

Example: An example of application could be a national tax authority implementing open data legislation to allow businesses and individuals to access and use tax data for various purposes, such as research, development of new services, or to enhance transparency and accountability in the tax system. This could involve the use of APIs to enable different systems and applications to interact with the tax data, ensuring behavioural interoperability.

LOST view: LV-Binding Power and Jurisdiction

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/TaxLegislationOnBehaviouralInteroperabilityGrouping

ABB name: egovera:TaxLegislationOnBehaviouralInteroperabilityGrouping

