      <property key="dct:modified" value="2023-11-20"/>
      <property key="dct:identifier" value="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/LegislationOnBehaviouralInteroperabilityGrouping"/>
      <property key="dct:title" value="Legislation On Behavioural Interoperability (NBI) Catalogue"/>
      <property key="dct:type" value="egovera:LegislationOnBehaviouralInteroperabilityGrouping"/>
      <property key="skos:definition" value="Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NBI) is a grouping that covers a set of laws, regulations, and guidelines aimed at ensuring the seamless interaction of different systems, applications, and services. It focuses on the behavior of systems when they interact, ensuring that they operate together effectively and efficiently. The NBI legislation is designed to facilitate the exchange of information and services among different systems, promoting interoperability and integration."/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSource" value="EIRA Team Research"/>
      <property key="eira:definitionSourceReference"/>
      <property key="skos:example" value="An example of the application of the Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NBI) is in the healthcare sector. Here, different systems such as electronic health records, hospital information systems, and telemedicine applications need to interact seamlessly to provide effective and efficient healthcare services. The NBI legislation ensures that these systems can exchange information and services in a predictable and consistent manner, promoting interoperability and integration."/>
      <property key="skos:note" value="The Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NBI) is an important aspect of information technology and telecommunications. It is concerned with the ability of different systems, applications, and services to work together seamlessly. This involves not only the technical aspects of interoperability but also the legal and regulatory aspects. The NBI legislation provides a framework for ensuring that systems behave in a predictable and consistent manner when they interact, facilitating the exchange of information and services. It covers a wide range of areas, including data exchange standards, communication protocols, interface specifications, and service level agreements."/>
      <property key="eira:concept" value="eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="LV-Architecture Principles"/>
      <property key="eira:view" value="LV-Binding Power and Jurisdiction"/>
      <property key="eira:eifLayer" value="Legal"/>
    </element>
    <element xsi:type="archimate:Grouping" name="Tax Digital Public Service Consumers Catalogue" id="id-6365da58c309452d9d02b92eec13bdcf">
      <documentation>Definition: Tax Digital Public Service Consumers Catalogue ABB is a Grouping that covers a range of business actors involved in the administration, regulation, and interaction with tax-related digital public services. These actors include social security administration, businesses, citizens, customs authorities, statistical authorities, quality control authorities, public administration, tributary justice courts, tax ombudsmen, temporary excise taxpayers, registered excise tax payers, and tax administration.

Source: EIRA Team Research

Additional information: The Tax Digital Public Service Consumers Catalogue ABB is a comprehensive list of all the business actors that interact with tax-related digital public services. These actors play various roles in the tax system, from administration and regulation to compliance and dispute resolution. The catalogue is designed to provide a clear understanding of the different roles and responsibilities in the tax system, and to facilitate the development and implementation of digital public services in the tax sector.

Example: For instance, the 'Social Security Administration' is a business actor responsible for administering social security programs, while the 'Business' actor represents businesses that interact with digital public services for tax purposes. 'Citizen' represents individual taxpayers who use digital public services for their tax affairs. 'Customs Authority' and 'Statistical Authorities' are involved in the regulation and monitoring of tax-related activities. 'Quality Control Authority' ensures the standards and compliance in tax-related activities. 'Public Administration' and 'Tax Administration' are overarching actors responsible for the overall administration and regulation of the tax system. 'Tributary Justice Court' and 'Tax Ombudsman' are involved in resolving tax-related disputes. 'Temporary Excise Taxpayers' and 'Registered Excise Tax Payer' represent different types of taxpayers in the excise tax system.

LOST view: OV-Digital Public Service Consumers

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/TaxDigitalPublicServiceConsumersCatalogueGrouping

ABB name: egovera:TaxDigitalPublicServiceConsumersCatalogueGrouping

EIRA concept: eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2023-08-21

